  (W, "This branch turns the keys of the first branch into something one person can hand to another. An address is a short string that a receiver gives to a payer so that the payer's wallet can build a transaction output that only the receiver can spend. In the book's story Alice wants to pay Bob, and the method she uses has to make sure that only Bob can later spend what he receives [BK]. The branch follows the history of the answer, from the first and simplest scheme of the original Bitcoin paper, in which Alice pays a public key directly, to the addresses that wallets display today."),
