  (W, "An output script is the field of a transaction output that states the conditions under which the funds of that output may be spent. The book describes the arrangement as follows: bitcoins are received to an output script that acts like a public key, and bitcoin spending is authorised by an input script that acts like a signature [BK]. The output script is therefore the lock, and the input script the key that opens it. In the specification of pay to script hash the same field is called by its internal name, the scriptPubKey [B16]."),
  (Y, "Because the lock is a program and not a fixed public key, the receiver can choose what must be shown to spend. The simplest lock asks for a signature that matches one public key. A richer lock can require a public key whose hash matches a stored value, several signatures, or a script that is revealed only when the funds are spent. All the address kinds of this chapter exist so that the receiver can choose among these locks and the payer can build the right one without understanding it."),
  (E, "Output scripts are met whenever a wallet or a block explorer displays an output. Bitcoin Core reports the script of an address when it is asked to validate it, and for the legacy address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy it gives the hexadecimal string 76a914bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b88ac [CORE]. Reading it byte by byte, 76 stands for the operation that duplicates the top item, a9 for the operation that hashes it, 14 says that twenty bytes of data follow, the twenty bytes are the commitment to the public key, and 88 and ac stand for the verification of equality and the check of the signature [BK]."),
