  (E, "Input scripts are met in every spending transaction. The three examples of the chapter show the pattern: the input script of a pay to public key output is the signature alone, that of a pay to public key hash output is the signature and the public key, and that of a pay to script hash output is the signatures followed by the whole redeem script [BK]. The specification of pay to script hash adds a restriction: validation fails if there are any operations other than pushing data in the input script [B16]."),
  (H, "When a node validates a spend, it takes the input script and the output script and runs them as one program. The book explains that you can combine them together, input script first, and then note that each piece of data, shown in angle brackets, is placed at the top of a list of items called a stack [BK]. The data items of the input script are therefore pushed first, and the operations of the output script act on them afterwards. In the pay to public key hash example the signature is pushed first and the public key is placed on top of it."),
