  (W, "A stack is a list of items to which items are added and from which items are removed at one end only, which is called the top. The script machine of Bitcoin evaluates a script on a stack. Data is placed on top of the stack; an operation code, abbreviated opcode, is a one-byte instruction that, when it is encountered, uses items from the stack, starting with the topmost items [BK]. Evaluation of the combined input and output script ends with a result: the script passes if there is a nonzero item on top of the stack at the end of evaluation [BK]."),
  (Y, "The design is simple on purpose. A machine with one stack and a short list of operations is easy to specify exactly, which a consensus system requires, because every node must reach the same verdict for every transaction. If all the scripts of a transaction pass, and all the other details of the transaction are valid, then full nodes consider the transaction valid [BK]. Anything that a node cannot evaluate in the same way as all others would split the network."),
  (E, "Every spend of every output is checked in this manner. The book walks through the two scripts of this chapter. In the pay to public key example, the signature is put on the stack and the public key on top of it; the opcode OP_CHECKSIG consumes two elements, starting with the public key and followed by the signature, verifies that the signature corresponds to the public key and commits to various fields of the transaction, and replaces itself on the stack with 1 if the signature is correct and with 0 if it is not [BK]."),
  (H, "The pay to public key hash example uses four more opcodes. OP_DUP duplicates the top item, so that the public key is on the stack twice. OP_HASH160 replaces the top item with its HASH160. The commitment that was stored in the output script is pushed. OP_EQUALVERIFY consumes the top two items and checks that they are equal, and if they are not, the whole script fails. What remains is the signature and the public key, which OP_CHECKSIG consumes as before [BK]. The chapter's checks run exactly this sequence on a list used as a stack, with the real HASH160 of the example key: with the right commitment the signature and the key remain, with a wrong one the script fails; and they confirm that an ECDSA signature made by the toolkit's sketch verifies against the key and fails for a different message."),
